<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kufa</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kufa was a garrison city founded by Arab Muslim forces in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> in the 630s during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun</a> conquests. It became a major political and religious centre and served as the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> Ali. Its population was central to the conflicts that ended the Rashidun Caliphate, and it remained a centre of Alid sympathy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kufa.jpg" alt="Kufa" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ali sabih kadhim · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D9%85%D8%B3%D8%AC%D8%AF_%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B3%D9%87%D9%84%D8%A9.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kufa lies on the banks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, about 170 kilometres south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> and 10 kilometres north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najaf">Najaf</a>. It was one of the two amsar, garrison cities, of Iraq, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a>, serving as a military base and administrative centre. It was founded in 637 or 638 as an encampment by the Companion Saʻd ibn Abī Waqqas beside the Lakhmid Arab city of al-Hirah, after the Arab victory at al-Qadisiyyah in 636 and the fall of the Persian Empire, and was laid out in a circular plan according to the Partho-Sasanian model, with seven divisions.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Kufa was restless from the start. In the 640s its commons complained that the governor shared out the spoils of war unfairly, and in 642 Umar summoned Saʻd to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>, found him innocent, but deposed him to avert ill feeling. Governors followed one another under Uthman, and in 656 Kufan agitators joined the Egyptian instigators who marched on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>. After Uthman was killed, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-ibn-abi-talib">Ali ibn Abi Talib</a> moved the capital from Medina to Kufa. He was attacked in the Great Mosque by the Kharijite Abd-al-Rahman ibn Muljam while praying.</p><p>In the Umayyad era Muawiyah I appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziyad-ibn-abihi">Ziyad ibn Abihi</a> governor and he put down opposition among the followers of Hasan. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazid-i">Yazid I</a> became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>, the Kufans turned to Muhammad's grandson Husayn for leadership, and Yazid sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ubayd-allah-ibn-ziyad">Ubayd Allah ibn Ziyad</a> as governor, which led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-karbala">Battle of Karbala</a>. In 749 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> under al-Hasan ibn Qahtaba took Kufa and made it their capital, before moving their seat to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> in 762. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qarmatians">Qarmatians</a> sacked it in 905, 924 and 927, and it never fully recovered. From the 11th century it came under constant attack, shrank and lost its importance.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Kufa is a holy city of Shia Islam, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najaf">Najaf</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-karbala">Karbala</a> and others. The Great Mosque contains the shrines of Muslim ibn Aqil, Hani ibn Urwa and al-Mukhtar, and the place where Ali was struck. Other sites include Ali's house and the tomb of Zayd ibn Ali. The city was a major centre of scholarship: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-hanifa">Abu Hanifa</a>, a founder of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> school of law, was a Kufan, and Shia theology began there. The Kufic script is named for the city, and is regarded as the earliest form of Arabic script, derived from the script of al-Hirah. Its population was estimated at 110,000 in 2003.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kufa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>This system kept conquered agriculture productive and channelled its surplus into stipends that sustained the Arab military establishment in purpose-built garrison cities, <b>Kufa</b> and Basra in Iraq and Fustat in Egypt, which were at once military camps, administrative centres and growing commercial hubs.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>He made Damascus the seat of the caliphate in place of Ali's base at <b>Kufa</b> and the earlier capital of Medina.</blockquote><a href="/empire/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The free Muslims of the towns, craftsmen, shopkeepers, porters, water-carriers and labourers, filled the markets of Baghdad, Basra, <b>Kufa</b> and the provincial capitals; Baghdad, founded in 762, was probably the largest city in the world until the 930s.</blockquote><a href="/societies/abbasid-caliphate">Social Hierarchy of the Abbasid Caliphate</a></li><li><blockquote>Wounded when Isa ibn Musa's Abbasid troops scattered his army at Bāchamrā south of <b>Kufa</b>, Ibrahim ibn Abdallah died there, ending the revolt he had led after his brother.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-14">14 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kharijite Ibn Muljam struck the fourth caliph, Ali ibn Abi Talib, with a poisoned sword as he prayed in the Great Mosque of <b>Kufa</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-26">26 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>His authority depended on governors and tribal allies, and he faced revolts from the Kharijites of the Najdiyya and from al-Mukhtar's pro-Alid movement at <b>Kufa</b>, which Mus'ab defeated in 687.</blockquote><a href="/state/caliphate-of-ibn-al-zubayr">Caliphate of Ibn al-Zubayr</a></li></ul></section>
